# What CPython prints for this program when the three names are the reference's own —
# `from math import floor, ceil, sqrt` on the first line, which is what the reference calls them
# (python3 3.12). gusty has them as builtins, so the oracle leg cannot run this file as written and
# the ledger records it `not_applicable`; the twin comparison is asserted in
# integration/math_names_test.go, which runs the same source with that import prefixed.
#
#   2
#   -3
#   3
#   -2
#   2
#   7
#   1
#   3.0
#   1.4142135623730951
#   3.5
#   [2, 3]
#   2
#   True
#
# The first eight lines are the row (roadmap Gap R.51, ADR 0264): `floor` and `ceil` answer a whole
# number, in the word a whole number travels in, on both backends. They used to answer `2.0`,
# `-3.0`, `3.0`, `-2.0` compiled and `NameError` interpreted — three engines, three behaviours, and
# the checker in the middle calling all of them well-typed. The rest is the answer travelling: beside
# a float (widened, not truncated), in a container, in an f-string, in a comparison.
print(floor(2.7))
print(floor(-2.7))
print(ceil(2.2))
print(ceil(-2.2))
print(floor(2.7))
print(floor(7))
print(ceil(True))
print(sqrt(9))
print(sqrt(2))
print(floor(2.7) + 1.5)
print([floor(2.7), ceil(2.2)])
print(f"{floor(2.7)}")
print(sqrt(4) == 2)
